Look at your hand and spread your fingers. Now picture each finger many times longer, with thin skin stretched between them, down to your ankle. That is a bat's wing. The thumb stays short and free, with a claw for clinging and climbing.

Because the wing has so many bones and joints, a bat can change its shape with every beat. A bird's wing is stiffer. This is one reason bats can twist and turn so sharply after insects. Long before bats, flying reptiles called [[@pterosaurs|pterosaurs]] also flew on wings of skin, but theirs hung mainly from one enormous finger. A bat's wing skin is thin but tough, and it heals quickly if torn. It is dotted with tiny hairs that seem to help the bat feel how the air is flowing over it.

{{story:pterosaurs}}

Without insects, the world would be buried in waste. Termites and beetles break down fallen wood. Fly maggots and beetles clear away dead animals. [[@power-dung-beetle|Dung beetles]] roll, bury and eat animal droppings, putting nutrients back into the ground.

Australia learned this the hard way. When settlers brought cattle, the native dung beetles, used to dry kangaroo droppings, could not cope with large, wet cow pats. Dung piled up on pastures and bush flies multiplied. From 1965, the Australian Dung Beetle Project brought in dozens of species from Africa and Europe. Many settled in, and pastures recovered.

{{story:honey}}

The bandleader W.C. Handy later wrote about a night around 1903, at a station in Tutwiler, [[@mississippi|Mississippi]]. Waiting for a delayed train, he heard a man pressing a knife against his guitar strings and sliding it, so the strings moaned. The man was singing about going "where the Southern cross the Dog", a place where two railway lines met.

Handy described it as the strangest music he had ever heard. He began writing down and arranging blues tunes. His "Memphis Blues" (1912) and "St. Louis Blues" (1914) became hits, and he became known as the Father of the Blues.

{{story:mississippi}}

Dating a cave painting is hard. Charcoal can be carbon-dated, but red ochre contains no carbon. So scientists use a clever trick. Water dripping down cave walls leaves thin crusts of a mineral called calcite. Calcite traps tiny amounts of uranium, which turns into thorium at a known rate. If a crust formed on top of a painting, the painting must be older than the crust.

This method showed that a red disc in El Castillo cave in [[@spain|Spain]] is more than 40,000 years old. Some scientists have argued that marks in other [[@caves-form|Spanish caves]] are over 64,000 years old, which would mean [[@neanderthals|Neanderthals]] made them. Others question those dates, and the debate continues.

{{story:stone-age}}

In 1947, the year India became independent, Raza joined a few young painters in Bombay to form the Progressive Artists' Group. Its members included F.N. Souza and [[@mf-husain|M.F. Husain]]. They wanted Indian art to be bold and modern, and to break away from both old-fashioned British academic painting and the gentle style of the Bengal School.

Their timing mattered. A new nation was asking what it wanted to look like, and these artists offered fresh answers. Several of them went on to become famous around the world.

{{story:circles}}

People questioned caste long before modern times. [[@buddha|The Buddha]] welcomed followers of every background. In the 12th century, [[@basavanna|Basavanna]] in Karnataka rejected caste rules. Poets like Kabir and Ravidas sang that God did not care about birth, and [[@mirabai-kabir|their verses]] are still sung today.

In 1848, Jyotirao and Savitribai Phule opened a school for girls in [[@pune|Pune]]. They later taught Dalit children too, which angered many in the city. In Kerala, Narayana Guru built temples open to all, and the Vaikom Satyagraha of 1924–25 demanded the right to walk on roads near a temple.

{{story:ambedkar}}

Barcelona is the success story people often point to. Before the 1992 Olympics, it rebuilt a run-down industrial waterfront into beaches, a marina and new neighbourhoods. Tourism grew enormously in the following decades, and the city became one of Europe's most visited.

For India, the 1982 Asian Games in Delhi brought new flyovers, stadiums and hotels. They also brought colour television: [[@tv-india|Doordarshan]] began broadcasting in colour that year so that people could watch the Games. Before the [[@commonwealth-games|2010 Commonwealth Games]], Delhi rapidly expanded its [[@metro-trains|metro]] network.

{{story:ancient-olympia}}

In many Indian fishing villages, men catch the fish and women sell it. Fisherwomen buy from the boats, then carry baskets to markets, sometimes travelling by bus or train. In Mumbai, Koli women in bright saris have sold fish in the city's markets for generations. The Kolis were among the first people to live on the seven islands that became [[@mumbai|Mumbai]].

Women also dry, salt and pickle fish. On some beaches, long rows of small fish lie drying on mats in the sun. Dried fish lasts for months and travels well, so it reaches villages far from the sea.

{{story:fishing-boats}}

In 1973, a religious leader from Kerala named Kesavananda Bharati challenged a land law. The case grew into a huge question: can [[@parliament-india|Parliament]] change any part of the Constitution it likes?

Thirteen judges heard it, the largest bench ever. By seven votes to six, they ruled that Parliament can amend the Constitution but cannot destroy its basic structure, such as [[@democracy-what|democracy]], the rule of law and fundamental rights. This idea still guides Indian law.

{{story:civil-rights}}

In the 1920s, the archaeologist Leonard Woolley dug up the royal tombs at Ur. He found beautiful game boards decorated with shell and red limestone, now in the British Museum. Each board had 20 squares, and players used pyramid-shaped dice.

For thousands of years nobody knew how to play. Then Irving Finkel, a curator at the British Museum, translated a clay tablet written by a Babylonian scholar in 177 BCE that explained the rules. The game had been played across the Middle East for about 3,000 years. Finkel later learned that Jewish families in [[@kochi|Kochi]], in [[@kerala|Kerala]], were still playing a version of it in the 20th century.

{{story:probability}}

Besides yellow, there are other prizes. The green jersey goes to the best sprinter, who collects points for fast finishes. The white jersey with red polka dots goes to the King of the Mountains, the best climber. The plain white jersey is for the best young rider, aged 25 or under.

Teams work together. Most riders, called domestiques, help their team leader by fetching water bottles, sheltering him from wind and setting a fast pace on climbs.

{{story:bicycle-balance}}

In the 700s and 800s, the Christian church in western Europe settled on 1 November as All Saints' Day, also called All Hallows. "Hallow" is an old word for a saint or holy person. The evening before became All Hallows' Eve, shortened over time to Halloween.

All Souls' Day, on 2 November, was set aside for praying for the dead. In parts of Britain, poor people and children went "souling", going door to door offering prayers in return for small cakes. Some historians see this as an ancestor of trick-or-treating.

{{story:bats-fly}}

In the 1960s, some people with severe epilepsy had their corpus callosum cut to stop seizures spreading. The American scientist Roger Sperry and his team tested these "split-brain" patients.

If a word was shown only to a patient's left hemisphere, they could say it. If it was shown only to the right, they couldn't name it, but their left hand could pick out the matching object. Each half seemed to have its own skills. Sperry won a Nobel Prize in 1981 for this work.

{{story:leonardo}}

Many reflexes protect you. You [[@blinking|blink]] when something flies at your face. You cough when food goes down the wrong way. You gag when something touches the back of your throat. You [[@sneezes|sneeze]] to clear your nose. In bright light, your pupils shrink to protect your eyes, and in the dark they open wide.

If you step on something sharp, you don't just lift that foot. Your other leg stiffens at the same time to hold you up. That's a reflex too, and it works without you thinking about which leg should do what. Your body runs hundreds of quieter reflexes all day too, adjusting your [[@heart-beat|heartbeat]], your breathing and the size of your blood vessels, without you ever noticing.

{{story:speed-distance}}

The finest walls at Machu Picchu are made of granite blocks, each shaped to fit its neighbours exactly. Builders pounded the stone with harder hammerstones and ground the faces smooth. They may have tried each block in place, lifted it off, trimmed it and tried again.

Walls lean slightly inwards. Doors and windows are trapezoid-shaped, wider at the bottom than the top. These shapes help the city stand firm in a land of frequent earthquakes. When the ground shakes, the stones can shift and settle back into place.

{{story:potatoes}}

Canada has two official languages, English and French. In the province of Quebec, French is the main language, and road signs, shops and schools mostly use it. Food packets all over Canada carry both languages, so a cereal box gives you a free French lesson at breakfast.

Long before either language arrived, [[indigenous|indigenous]] peoples lived here, and they still do: First Nations, Inuit and Métis. In 1999 Canada created a new territory in the far north, Nunavut, where most people are Inuit and Inuktitut is an official language. Canada has also faced up to painful history, such as residential schools where indigenous children were taken from their families.

{{story:aurora}}

Jai Singh II loved astronomy. In the 1720s and 1730s he built five observatories, called [[@jantar-mantar|Jantar Mantar]], in Jaipur, Delhi, [[@varanasi|Varanasi]], Ujjain and Mathura. The one in Jaipur is the largest. Its instruments are made of stone and marble, big enough to walk through.

The Samrat Yantra is a giant sundial about 27 m tall. Its shadow moves across the marked scale at about 1 mm every second, so you can watch [[@time-measure|time]] passing. Astronomers used the instruments to track the Sun, Moon and stars, and to make calendars.

{{story:time-measure}}

Glass was heavy and broke easily on bumpy roads and rolling ships. In Britain, Peter Durand patented the use of tin-plated iron containers in 1810. Bryan Donkin and John Hall bought the idea and opened a canning factory in London around 1812 or 1813. Their cans of beef and vegetables went on navy ships and Arctic expeditions.

These early cans were heavy and thick. A label from the time told people to open them with a chisel and hammer. Proper can openers only arrived in the 1850s, and the simple turning-wheel opener most kitchens use came later still.

{{story:napoleon}}

Leave a wet dough lying around and [[@food-science-bread-rise|wild yeast from the air]] will start to grow in it. The yeast eats sugars and [[@burps|burps out carbon dioxide]]. The gluten net catches the bubbles, and the dough swells. This kind of [[@fermentation|fermentation]] was probably discovered by accident.

Ancient Egyptians baked many kinds of risen bread in clay pots. Bread was so central to life that it was a kind of pay. Records and the remains of bakeries near Giza show that pyramid workers received bread and beer as part of their wages.

{{story:ancient-egypt}}
